{"id":65,"course_id":17,"title":"AWS Operational Excellence and Ownership","summary":"Translate AWS service sprawl into owned, observable, reviewable operations.","sequence":1,"body":"## Mental model\nOperational excellence means teams know who owns each workload, how changes are made, which signals matter, what risks are accepted, and how lessons improve the system. In AWS interviews, strong answers connect account structure, IAM, networking, telemetry, and recovery into one operating model.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice writing an ownership map for one service: account, region, VPC, load balancer, compute, database, DNS, alarms, runbook, deploy path, and escalation owner.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nCreate an AWS operations readiness review with owner, blast radius, backup expectation, alarm strategy, deployment path, and break-glass notes.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","practice_notes":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-aws-operations-foundations","course_category":"AWS Operations","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":321,"term":"AWS Operations Foundations for Platform Engineers","context":"AWS Operations","definition":"Operate AWS-backed platforms through Well-Architected thinking, CloudWatch signals, VPC request paths, load balancer health, backups, and change safety."},{"id":322,"term":"Evidence","context":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":323,"term":"Blast radius","context":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":324,"term":"Rollback","context":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":325,"term":"Runbook","context":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":193,"lesson_id":65,"prompt":"What is the main goal of AWS Operational Excellence and Ownership?","answer":"Translate AWS service sprawl into owned, observable, reviewable operations.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":194,"lesson_id":65,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":195,"lesson_id":65,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
